{"rule": "Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and, in the text field, an original fictional passage of about 250 words that comes immediately AFTER the current text in the same invented story.", "text": "\"Wenna,\" Mara whispered. \"Your arm.\" The old woman tugged her sleeve down, but not before Mara saw the thread pulse once, faintly, like a heartbeat that wasn't hers. \"It's nothing a cup of nettle tea won't settle,\" Wenna said, though her voice had lost its iron. She lowered herself onto the stool by the hearth, and for the first time Mara noticed how thin her hands were, how the knuckles stood out like knots in rope. Tobias stirred against Mara's chest. \"Mara?\" His voice was small and hoarse, but it was his. \"Why is it so cold?\" \"Hush now. You were dreaming.\" She pulled the blanket from the bed and wrapped it around him twice, tucking the ends beneath his chin the way Mam used to. Outside, the laughter had stopped. The silence it left behind was worse. Even the wind had died, and the candles burned straight and still, as if holding their breath. \"It'll come back,\" Mara said. It wasn't a question. Wenna stared into the embers. \"It's already here, child. It came in with the thread. It took what I offered, and it'll take the rest in its own time.\" \"Then we'll cut it off you. The way you cut his.\" \"Salt only works once on the same blood.\" Wenna finally looked up, and her eyes were wet and very clear. \"Which is why, come morning, you're going to walk to the marsh chapel and find the one who taught me. If she's still breathing.\""}
